  "rationale": "The primary standard forms differ: Indonesian lemari and Malaysian Malay almari. However, the national split is not exclusive: Indonesian lists almari as a nonstandard variant, while Malaysian Malay records lemari as colloquial for almari. Kabinet is also an overlapping, narrower storage-furniture term. The English cue and both answers are broadly sound, but the blank contrast omits an important qualification about cross-variety overlap and register.",
  "claim_reviews": [
    {
      "subject": "English cue and sense",
      "conclusion": "Accurate. Cupboard commonly denotes furniture with doors and usually shelves for storing items, matching the supplied sense.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/cupboard",
        "https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/us/definition/english/cupboard"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Indonesian answer: lemari",
      "conclusion": "Correct as the standard Indonesian form for a large storage cabinet; almari is listed as a nonstandard form.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://kbbi.kemendikdasmen.go.id/entri/lemari"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Malaysian Malay answer: almari",
      "conclusion": "Correct as the standard Malaysian Malay form for a tiered, usually doored storage cupboard.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=175768&keyword=almari"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "National split and overlap",
      "conclusion": "The split is useful but not exclusive. Malaysian Malay records lemari as a colloquial equivalent of almari, while Indonesian treats almari as nonstandard. This is register asymmetry, not a clean absence of cross-variety overlap.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=73980&keyword=lemari",
        "https://kbbi.kemendikdasmen.go.id/entri/lemari"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Shared alternative kabinet",
      "conclusion": "Kabinet overlaps across both varieties for particular cabinet/storage contexts, but dictionary definitions are narrower than generic cupboard and do not invalidate lemari versus almari as the primary contrast.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=115704&keyword=kabinet",
        "https://kbbi.web.id/kabinet"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Final relation",
      "conclusion": "Different-common-word is defensible only with a qualification that the forms are related and have cross-variety colloquial/nonstandard overlap; an unqualified exclusive contrast would be overstated.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=73980&keyword=lemari",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=175768&keyword=almari",
        "https://kbbi.kemendikdasmen.go.id/entri/lemari"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "False-friend contrast",
      "conclusion": "Missing. Add a contrast stating that Indonesian standard lemari corresponds to Malaysian Malay almari, while almari is nonstandard in Indonesian and lemari is colloquial in Malaysian Malay; kabinet is a context-limited shared alternative.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_limitation": "The cited dictionary entries establish meanings and register labels but do not quantify everyday frequency across speakers."
    }
  ]
